  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Scattered showers and thunderstorms are \nanticipated to develop across portions of eastern Pennsylvania \nand New Jersey later today into tonight. Temperatures will be \nwarmer with increasing humidity as well. \n\nAs high pressure shifts offshore through the day, southwesterly\nflow will ramp up into the afternoon. Temperatures will warm\ninto the mid 80s for most areas (even some upper 80s in spots),\non the order of 5 degrees warmer than Saturday. Dewpoints will\nalso surge back well into the 60s to around 70 degrees by the\nafternoon hours, making for a noticeable return in summer-like\nhumidity. Skies will turn mostly cloudy by the afternoon,\nespecially along and NW of I-95, which should help negate the\ndegree of unpleasant humidity.\n\nThe main forecast challenge for today will be the potential for\nscattered showers and thunderstorms developing later this\nafternoon and into tonight. The latest trend in the CAMs has\nbeen for an even later convective initiation time than\nbefore, which will significantly limit any potential for severe\nand flash flood threats. The greatest limiting factor appears \nto be a lack of substantial instability until close to sunset, \nwith even the highest modeled CAPE largely less than 1,000 \nJ/kg. This combined with poor lapse rates and weak forcing \nduring the peak diurnal time frame should limit coverage for \nmuch of the daytime hours. However, better forcing does look to \narrive during the evening to even overnight hours. So at least\nsome scattered showers and potential for a few thunderstorms are\nstill anticipated to develop at some point. We still cannot \ntotally rule out a couple stronger storms, with gusty winds, \nsmall hail, and brief heavy downpours possible.\n\nScattered showers may persist well into the overnight hours as\nforcing remains aloft.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Heat and humidity will build into the middle of\nthe week, along with daily chances for showers and \nthunderstorms. There is an increasing threat of severe \nthunderstorms during the middle of the week, namely Tuesday\nthrough Thursday.\n\nBroad upper level ridging returns to the eastern US early next \nweek, bringing in surface high pressure across the eastern US. \nAfternoon highs will creep back into the mid 80s to low 90s for\nmost areas, about 5 degrees above average for early September. \nDewpoints will also remain in the upper 60s to low 70s. The\nhottest day looks to be Tuesday, when high temperatures could\nget into the mid 90s across Delmarva and into the Philly metro.\nResulting heat indices will be well into the 90s to low 100s. We\nwill have to keep an eye on the potential for Heat Advisories.\n\nOne other aspect to watch with this ridging pattern is the \npotential for thunderstorms to rotate around the periphery of \nthe ridge and the position of a frontal boundary during the\nmiddle of the week. Guidance is coming into a consensus that the\npattern will be supportive of multiple rounds of thunderstorms,\nespecially during the Tuesday through Thursday time frame. We\ncould see some scattered showers and storms on Monday, though\nthe severe and flash flood risks appear to be low for Monday.\n\nSPC maintains 15% probabilities for severe thunderstorms for \nour region on Wednesday (Day 4). As of this morning, SPC has \nintroduced a Day 3 SLIGHT Risk (level 2 out of 5) for Tuesday\n(Day 3), and introduced 15% probabilities for Thursday (Day 5) \nas well. The northwest flow pattern that will be in place will \nbe supportive of all severe threats, including damaging winds,\nlarge hail, and tornadoes, though the threat of flash flooding \nis typically lower in these patterns. Long story short, the \nTuesday through Thursday period looks very active with multiple \nrounds of thunderstorms across the region, but details remain \nunclear. Stay tuned for updates on the threat for severe \nthunderstorms in the coming days.\n\nBy Friday, current model consensus indicates the frontal \nboundary will be pushing south of our region with Canadian high\npressure building across the Northeast, which would bring an \nend to the risk of thunderstorms and severe weather. Some storms\ncould linger toward our south, especially near Delmarva on\nFriday though. This extended range pattern also supports dry \nand pleasant weather for Labor Day weekend.",
